{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BmUbMGTTZJtYA29vnCEKej","type":"tool_result","content":"=== 01_single_run_shap_reliability_under_evaluation_budgets\n64:_INFL = float(np.sqrt(2.0))  # predictive spread: observed-run noise + rerun noise\n110:    draws = phi[None, :] + _INFL * noise\n\"\"\"Baseline candidate: single-run reliability diagnostic for stochastic Kernel SHAP.\n\nInterface (python_rpc_v1:handle): ``handle(request) -> response`` with finite JSON-compatible\nvalues only.  Candidate state lives only in this module's memory; there is no shared file system\nwith the evaluator, so nothing may be read from or written to evaluator paths.\n\nTask.  The evaluator trains a tabular classifier (logistic regression, random forest or sklearn\nMLP) and runs a *stochastic Kernel SHAP* explainer (interventional value function, 32 background\nrows, fixed coalition budget n_masks per run) on explained instances.  For every instance you are\nshown ONE run of the explainer.  Predict, for each instance, the probability-like reliability\nscore r_hat in [0, 1] that independent reruns of the same explainer (same model, same instance,\nsame budget, fresh coalition sampling) reproduce this run's top-k attribution set.  The evaluator\nscores r_hat against y = mean over 8 hidden reruns of Jaccard(top-k(|phi|) of this run, top-k(|phi|)\nof the rerun) with k = 3, using squared error.  You never see labels, reruns, model internals\nor dataset identities.\n\nOperations\n----------\n{\"op\": \"reset\", \"seed\": <int>}\n    Start a fresh episode; seeds candidate-side randomness only.  Any non-negative integer seed\n    must be accepted.  The seed never identifies the workload.  Response: exactly {\"ok\": true}.\n\n{\"op\": \"diagnose\", \"workload\": {...}, \"instances\": [...]}\n    Exactly one diagnose call follows each reset.\n    workload (same field names in every plane):\n        \"n_features\": int d (number of ORIGINAL features, coalition masks have d columns)\n        \"n_masks\": int B (coalitions in the run; B = 4*d or 16*d, complement pairs adjacent)\n        \"family\": \"logreg\" | \"random_forest\" | \"mlp\"\n        \"k\": 3 (size of the top-k attribution set)\n        \"n_instances\": int, equals len(instances) (<= 20)\n=== 02_locally_calibrated_rerun_simulation_for_single_run_kernel_shap_reliability\n35:_FOLDS = 8\n36:_N_DRAWS = 1000  # even: antithetic pairs\n42:# s_f141 (full cov, mult sqrt(2)), lbd = log2(B/d) - 3 (0 at B=8d midpoint), mz = tanh(margin z / 3),\n118:    draws = phi[None, :] + mult * noise\n155:    fold = rng.permutation(pairs) % _FOLDS\n157:    phi, loo, dev, n = _jackknife_cov(Z, w, v, v0, v1, fold_of_row, _FOLDS)\n178:    half = _N_DRAWS // 2\n179:    zf = g.standard_normal((half, _FOLDS))\n\"\"\"Candidate: calibrated, low-MC-noise rerun simulation for single-run Kernel SHAP reliability.\n\nInterface (python_rpc_v1:handle): ``handle(request) -> response`` with finite JSON-compatible\nvalues only.  Candidate state lives only in this module's memory; nothing is read from or written\nto evaluator paths.\n\nOperations\n----------\n{\"op\": \"reset\", \"seed\": <int>}      -> exactly {\"ok\": true}; any non-negative integer seed.\n{\"op\": \"diagnose\", \"workload\": {...}, \"instances\": [...]}\n    workload: n_features d, n_masks B (4d or 16d, complement pairs adjacent), family\n    (\"logreg\" | \"random_forest\" | \"mlp\"), k (=3), n_instances.\n    instance keys: masks (bit-packed uint8, base64), weights (float64 [B]), values (float64 [B]),\n    anchors {\"empty\", \"full\"}.\n    Response: exactly {\"r_hat\": [float, ...]}, len == n_instances, finite, in [0, 1].\n\nMethod.  Same skeleton as the baseline: delete-one-group jackknife over complement pairs, then\nsimulate reruns by drawing attributions around the observed run and averaging the Jaccard overlap\nof the simulated top-k sets with the observed one.  Differences from the baseline, each kept\nseparate and individually switchable so they can be ablated:\n  1. Monte-Carlo noise reduction: 1000 draws (500 antithetic pairs) with common random numbers\n     fixed per episode via np.random.default_rng(seed).\n  2. A full-covariance simulator (``_jackknife_cov``; draws are phi + L z with L built from the\n     centred delete-one-group replicates, so the sum(phi) constraint is preserved) next to the\n     baseline diagonal simulator; spread multiplier ``mult`` is a parameter of ``_sim_jaccard``.\n  3. A small monotone ``_calibrate`` map (``_CALIB_COEF``) over the simulated Jaccards at several\n     spread multipliers, log2(B/d), a standardised top-k margin and the family, shrunk toward the\n     baseline-equivalent diagonal simulator.  See NOTES.md for how the constants were chosen.\n\"\"\"\nimport base64\nShell cwd was reset to /workspace/paper","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a0aea7f-e3c0-4bf6-b0cd-dac6f7fb95fd","uuid":"8126f863-c2e5-4012-8cf0-1321f57649dc","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:20:18.559Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== 01_single_run_shap_reliability_under_evaluation_budgets\n64:_INFL = float(np.sqrt(2.0))  # predictive spread: observed-run noise + rerun noise\n110:    draws = phi[None, :] + _INFL * noise\n\"\"\"Baseline candidate: single-run reliability diagnostic for stochastic Kernel SHAP.\n\nInterface (python_rpc_v1:handle): ``handle(request) -> response`` with finite JSON-compatible\nvalues only.  Candidate state lives only in this module's memory; there is no shared file system\nwith the evaluator, so nothing may be read from or written to evaluator paths.\n\nTask.  The evaluator trains a tabular classifier (logistic regression, random forest or sklearn\nMLP) and runs a *stochastic Kernel SHAP* explainer (interventional value function, 32 background\nrows, fixed coalition budget n_masks per run) on explained instances.  For every instance you are\nshown ONE run of the explainer.  Predict, for each instance, the probability-like reliability\n